{"id": 126993, "acmi_id": "", "title": "Adventure Island puppets", "title_zh_hans": "", "title_pa": "", "title_hi": "", "title_annotation": "", "title_annotation_zh_hans": "", "title_annotation_pa": "", "title_annotation_hi": "", "slug": "adventure-island", "creator_credit": "", "credit_line": "<p>Courtesy Arts Centre Melbourne</p>\n<p>Gift of ABC, 1997</p>", "credit_line_zh_hans": "", "credit_line_pa": "", "credit_line_hi": "", "headline_credit": "", "video_links": [], "record_type": "group", "type": "Object", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "go_live_at": null, "external": false, "first_production_date": null, "commissioned": false, "public_domain": false, "external_references": [], "brief_description": "<p>Adventure Island was a hugely popular ABC children’s television series, produced in Melbourne and screened five afternoons a week from 1967 to 1972. Created by Godfrey Philipp and John-Michael Howson, it grew out of The Magic Circle Club and followed stories set “far across the sea” in the Kingdom of Diddley-Dum-Diddley.</p>\n<p>Puppets helped connect the fantasy world to the studio. Characters including Maxie Mouse, Samson the Cat, Crispian Cockatoo and Gracie Galah chatted with the host between story chapters, often sharing viewer drawings, riddles and jokes.</p>\n<p>Built by Axel Axelrad, the puppets used different forms of operation. Samson was a large hand puppet, while the others were mechanical creations with rods inside or underneath the body to open the mouth or beak.</p>", "constellations_primary": [], "constellations_other": [], "recommendations": [], "creator_credit_for_label": "", "creator_credit_for_label_zh_hans": "", "creator_credit_for_label_pa": "", "creator_credit_for_label_hi": "", "headline_credit_for_label": "", "headline_credit_for_label_zh_hans": "", "headline_credit_for_label_pa": "", "headline_credit_for